Provide an axiomatic foundation for algorithmic information theory. Concretely: identify a finite list of axioms on a function $C: \Sigma^* \to \mathbb{N}$ (or on a family of such functions indexed by partial-recursive machines), where $\Sigma^*$ is the set of finite binary strings, such that:

(i) the axioms are satisfied by the standard plain Kolmogorov complexity $C(x) = \min\{|p| : U(p) = x\}$ and the prefix-free Kolmogorov complexity $K(x)$, where $U$ ranges over (prefix-free) universal Turing machines;

(ii) any function $C'$ satisfying the axioms is equivalent, up to an additive constant, to the standard Kolmogorov complexity (an invariance theorem of the same type as the classical Solomonoff–Kolmogorov–Chaitin invariance theorem follows from the axioms);

(iii) the principal theorems of algorithmic information theory — including the invariance theorem, the coding theorem, the existence of Martin-Löf random sequences, Levin's symmetry of information, the incompressibility method, and the basic inequalities relating $C$, $K$, and conditional complexities — are derivable from the axioms without reference to a specific model of computation.

Produce such an axiom system, prove the corresponding invariance and structural theorems, and characterize the class of all complexity measures it captures.

